╭─────────────────────────────── Round 1 · CRITIC (Conf: 0.78, Ready: False) ────────────────────────────────╮
│ • [C1 -> A2] (CHALLENGED): The timeline joins Engagement, Mission, Project and Contact rows, but a PM sees │
│ a row only if they can open the underlying record. Row-level checks are ambiguous when the records have    │
│ different permissions. Example: a PM on Mission X for Country Y can open the Mission, but the linked       │
│ Engagement is a confidential ministerial meeting held under another team's access. Rows could be shown     │
│ with partial data, or a contact name could be shown while the engagement is hidden. Counts, the 'N         │
│ engagements' summary in the New Mission flow, the 'Active missions' banner and pagination totals would     │
│ also leak the existence of restricted records. The criterion 'never displays a row the user cannot open'   │
│ does not cover aggregates, hidden-row gaps, or the case where the Mission is visible but the linked        │
│ Contact is not. Also, 'some access to Country records' means a PM with access to one mission in a country  │
│ could see the full history of every other team's engagements there. That breaks least-privilege and lets a │
│ PM see another country team's notes.                                                                       │
│   Eval: Round 1: the Proposer defers to the existing access model, which assumes it already enforces       │
│ least-privilege at record level and works when composed across entities. That is unverified.               │
│ • [C2 -> A6] (CHALLENGED): Contacts are shown with their current record data next to historical engagement │
│ dates. If a minister or ministry official has been replaced, a 2022 engagement row will show the new       │
│ person's name and title against a meeting they never attended. This misleads the PM about who the          │
│ counterpart was. PMs may then reach out to the wrong ministry contact or assume the wrong relationship     │
│ exists. A deactivated or departed contact may also be displayed with no stale marker. Across ~100          │
│ countries with frequent turnover, this is the main failure mode of the feature, not an edge case. A cheap  │
│ mitigation exists without a new schema: store or show the contact name as recorded on the engagement, or   │
│ flag rows where the contact record was modified or deactivated after the engagement date.                  │
│   Eval: Round 1: the Proposer accepts that history shows current contact data and calls this acceptable    │
│ for v1, but gives no mitigation for misattribution.                                                        │
│ • [C3 -> A4] (CHALLENGED): The proposal says rows with missing country links are shown as gaps, but a      │
│ record can only be shown on a country timeline if the query can reach it. An Engagement that lacks a       │
│ direct country_id and has no Contact, Mission or Project link will be silently excluded. An Engagement     │
│ linked via a Contact is attributed to the Contact's current country, not the country where the engagement  │
│ took place. After a contact moves ministries or countries, past engagements may move to the wrong timeline │
│ or vanish from the right one. A PM who sees a clean 'no prior engagement' state for a country may start a  │
│ mission that duplicates or conflicts with earlier outreach. The 'single query' claim also hides multi-path │
│ attribution, which can cause double-counted or missing rows. The panel is labelled 'full history' but has  │
│ no completeness indicator, such as a count of unlinked records or a data-quality caveat. This makes false  │
│ reassurance the likely outcome.                                                                            │
│   Eval: Round 1: the Proposer treats gaps as 'visible', but records that fail the join are invisible by    │
│ construction. The claim does not hold.                                                                     │
│ • [C4 -> A3] (CHALLENGED): The panel is described as advisory and non-blocking, but the 'Active missions   │
│ in this country' banner is the only signal of an existing mission in the same country. Where the PM lacks  │
│ access to another team's mission, that mission is filtered out under the permission-inheritance rule. The  │
│ banner will then show nothing, which tells the PM that no one else is working there. Two teams can then    │
│ approach the same ministry in parallel with conflicting asks, a diplomatic risk. The empty state and the   │
│ banner need to distinguish 'none exist' from 'none visible to you', for example a count-only or            │
│ 'restricted mission exists' indicator. That indicator in turn has to be reconciled with the                │
│ confidentiality concern in C1. The design must choose one behaviour deliberately rather than by accident.  │
│   Eval: Round 1: the Proposer frames the feature as orientation, not a control, but the banner is used as  │
│ a safety signal and fails silently under least-privilege filtering.                                        │
│ • [C5 -> A5] (CHALLENGED): The proposal assumes each country has hundreds to low thousands of rows, with a │
│ 2-second p95 target for the largest country. Permission filtering is applied per row across the            │
│ Engagement, Mission and Contact joins, so the query cannot rely on the country_id and date indexes alone.  │
│ If access checks run in application code after the fetch, pagination breaks: a page of 50 can return 12    │
│ visible rows, or total counts are computed before filtering and leak or mislead. Large countries with long │
│ histories and heavy restricted content will be the slowest and most inconsistent. No data volume has       │
│ actually been measured, and the free-text 'summary' field is rendered with no check for sensitive notes    │
│ appearing in the timeline view.                                                                            │
│   Eval: Round 1: the Proposer's performance assumption ignores the cost and pagination effects of per-row  │
│ permission enforcement.                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                            │
