│ • [C1 -> A2] (ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN): Engineering gaps are closed: the permission predicate runs in the same  │
│ query as rows, totals and pagination, rows appear only if the primary record is openable, and linked       │
│ Contact/Project names are replaced by 'Restricted' placeholders. What remains is whether a PM with access  │
│ to one mission in a country should see other teams' engagements in that country. That is an institutional  │
│ access and classification decision that engineering cannot settle.                                         │
│   Eval: The Proposer fixed the aggregate-leak, partial-row and hidden-contact cases and correctly declined │
│ to invent new RBAC. It routed the cross-team visibility question to a human policy owner. Further argument │
│ here would not help.                                                                                       │
│ • [C2 -> A6] (ACCEPTED): The risk was a 2022 engagement row showing a replaced minister's successor as the │
│ counterpart. The Proposer now flags any row whose Contact was modified or deactivated after the engagement │
│ date, with 'may differ' wording and a 'Deactivated' tag, and covers it with a fixture test.                │
│   Eval: The revision uses existing updated_at and status fields and adds no schema or coordinator upkeep.  │
│ It over-flags harmless edits, which could cause some alert fatigue, but the wording is non-alarming and    │
│ over-flagging is the safe direction for an MVP. The risk is adequately bounded.                            │
│ • [C3 -> A4] (CHALLENGED): The static caveat, empty-state wording and deterministic precedence with dedupe │
│ resolve the false-reassurance and double-count concerns. One solvable gap remains. An Engagement           │
│ attributed only via the Contact fallback lands on the Contact's current country. If a ministry official    │
│ moved countries or posts, a past engagement from Country A appears in Country B's timeline as if it        │
│ happened there, and may drop out of Country A's. A PM preparing a mission to B may misread that history.   │
│ The row looks identical to directly attributed rows.                                                       │
│   Eval: The defense is sound on unreachable records, which cannot be counted by definition, and on the     │
│ caveat. It does not address how contact-derived rows are shown. The fix is a display-only label on rows    │
│ attributed via the fallback, such as 'Linked via contact's current country — may not reflect where this    │
│ took place'. That needs no schema change, backfill or coordinator work.                                    │
│ • [C4 -> A3] (ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN): The banner no longer implies that nobody else is working in the         │
│ country. It adds a count-only line that reveals no name, team or details. Whether disclosing that a        │
│ restricted active mission exists is acceptable is a diplomatic and security-classification decision.       │
│   Eval: The Proposer made a deliberate design choice, kept it to the minimum disclosure, and explicitly    │
│ flagged it for a human owner with a fallback to remove the line. Engineering cannot settle the remaining   │
│ question.                                                                                                  │
│ • [C5 -> A5] (ACCEPTED): The risks were post-fetch filtering breaking pagination and totals, unmeasured    │
│ volume, and summary text exposure. The permission predicate now runs in the same query with keyset         │
│ pagination on (date, id), and a production-sized measurement gate with a realistic restricted-row mix is a │
│ release criterion. Summary visibility is tied to the openable Engagement.                                  │
│   Eval: The defense removes the pagination and count inconsistency, and the measurement gate bounds the    │
│ performance risk cheaply. Summary text carries the same access as the record the user can already open, so │
│ it adds no new exposure. Redaction policy is a classification matter outside this MVP.                     │
│                                                                                                            │
